**Sec. 40001**
This section appropriates $24.5935 billion to the Coast Guard for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to procure operational assets and improve shore infrastructure. Key allocations include $4.3 billion each for Offshore Patrol Cutters and Polar Security Cutters, $3.5 billion for Arctic Security Cutters, and $2.283 billion for rotary-wing aircraft. Additional funds support fixed-wing aircraft ($1.1425 billion), Fast Response Cutters ($1 billion), and $4.379 billion for shore facility improvements, including boot camp barracks and the Coast Guard Yard. The bill also provides $2.2 billion for depot maintenance, $170 million for maritime domain awareness, and $75 million for autonomous maritime systems.

**Sec. 40002**
This section extends the Federal Communications Commission’s general auction authority to September 30, 2034, excluding specific bands between 3.1–3.45 GHz and 7.4–8.4 GHz. The Commission must auction at least 300 megahertz of spectrum, including 100 megahertz in the 3.98–4.2 GHz band within two years, while the Assistant Secretary of Commerce identifies 500 megahertz of federal spectrum for reallocation. Auctions for the reallocated spectrum must complete at least 200 megahertz within four years and the remainder within eight years. The Department of Commerce receives $50 million to conduct spectrum analysis and publish biennial reports on federal spectrum value.

**Sec. 40003**
This section appropriates approximately $12.5 billion to the Federal Aviation Administration for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to modernize air traffic control infrastructure. Major expenditures include $4.75 billion for telecommunications, $3 billion for radar replacement, and $1.9 billion to construct a new Air Route Traffic Control Center (ARTCC) while divesting at least three existing centers. Additional funds support the consolidation of at least 10 ARTCCs ($100 million), recapitalization of Terminal Radar Approach Control facilities ($1 billion), and weather observation systems ($80 million). The FAA must submit quarterly reports to Congress detailing expenditures under this section.

**Sec. 40004**
This section establishes user fees for space launch and reentry licenses issued by the Secretary of Transportation, starting in 2026, calculated as the lesser of a per-pound payload fee or a flat annual fee. The per-pound fee rises from $0.25 in 2026 to $1.50 in 2033, while the flat fee increases from $30,000 in 2026 to $200,000 in 2033, with both indexed to inflation thereafter. Fees are deposited into the newly created Office of Commercial Space Transportation Launch and Reentry Licensing and Permitting Fund. Seventy percent of these funds are made available without further appropriation to support the Office of Commercial Space Transportation.

**Sec. 40005**
This section appropriates $9.995 billion to NASA for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2032, to fund Mars missions, Artemis missions, and the Moon to Mars program. Key allocations include $4.1 billion for Space Launch System operations for Artemis IV and V, $2.6 billion for the Gateway lunar station, and $700 million for a commercial Mars telecommunications orbiter. Additional funds support International Space Station operations ($1.25 billion), Orion spacecraft procurement ($20 million), and $1 billion for infrastructure improvements at NASA centers like Kennedy and Johnson. At least 50% of these funds must be obligated by September 30, 2028, with all outlays completed by September 30, 2034.

**Sec. 40006**
This section amends 49 U.S.C. § 32912 to reduce the civil penalty for Corporate Average Fuel Economy (CAFE) violations from $5 to $0.00 per tenth of a mile per gallon shortfall. The amendment applies to all model years for which the Secretary of Transportation has not yet notified manufacturers of penalties due. This effectively eliminates financial penalties for manufacturers failing to meet CAFE standards for unnotified model years.

**Sec. 40007**
This section amends 49 U.S.C. § 49104(b) to increase the annual lease payment from the Metropolitan Washington Airports Authority to the U.S. Treasury. Payments remain at $3 million in 1987 dollars through 2026 but increase to $15 million in 2027 dollars for 2027 and subsequent years. The Secretary of Transportation and the Airports Authority must renegotiate lease payments at least every 10 years to ensure the amount never falls below $15 million in 2027 dollars.

**Sec. 40008**
This section rescinds any unobligated balances of amounts appropriated or made available by sections 40001, 40002, 40003, and 40004 of Public Law 117-169. These funds were originally designated for the National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration.

**Sec. 40009**
This section amends the Travel Promotion Act of 2009 to reduce the annual transfer from the Travel Promotion Fund from $100 million to $20 million. This reduction applies to the annual funding provided to the Corporation for Travel Promotion.

**Sec. 40010**
This section rescinds any unobligated balances of amounts made available by section 40007(a) of title IV of Public Law 117-169. These funds were originally designated for alternative fuel and low-emission aviation technology.

**Sec. 40011**
This section permanently rescinds $850 million of unobligated balances from the Public Wireless Supply Chain Innovation Fund. These funds were originally made available under section 106(a) of the CHIPS Act of 2022. [end of text]


